<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suppressions PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD SuppressionFilter Configuration 1.2//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/suppressions_1_2.dtd">

<!--
=============================================================================
config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Purpose:
  The companion file to config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, and the single place
  in this repository where an exemption from the Java documentation gate may be
  recorded. It is read by the SuppressionFilter that checkstyle.xml declares,
  addressed as ${config_loc}/suppressions.xml so that it resolves identically
  from the services/ aggregator and from each of the nine module directories one
  level deeper.

  It currently declares NO exemption. Every Java source under services/ is
  audited at full strength.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Why this file must exist even while it exempts nothing.
  Assumptions: it is NOT optional and it is not a placeholder. checkstyle.xml
    declares its SuppressionFilter with optional="false", which makes a missing
    companion an initialisation error rather than a silent pass: Checkstyle
    stops with "cannot initialize module SuppressionFilter - Unable to find:
    .../config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml", and because services/pom.xml binds
    the gate to the Maven validate phase, that error fails the whole nine-module
    reactor before a single class is compiled. The ruleset documents that
    fail-closed choice as deliberate, on the grounds the house states as "a run
    that verifies NOTHING must not read as green", and services/pom.xml states
    from its side that the flag "is relied upon and must not be worked around".
    An empty-but-present companion is therefore the only resolution that honours
    both files: the gate keeps failing closed, and it can actually run.

  Alternatives Considered: three other ways to make the reactor build were
    available and each was rejected for a specific reason.
      1. Flip optional to "true" in checkstyle.xml. Rejected because it removes
         the property both sibling files rely on: an absent or misspelled
         companion would then read as "accept all audit events", so the gate
         would report success having checked nothing.
      2. Set the plugin's own suppressions-location in services/pom.xml.
         Rejected for the reason recorded in both siblings: it splits one
         behaviour across two files, leaving the ruleset naming one companion
         while the POM names another and no way to tell which one won.
      3. Delete the SuppressionFilter module. Rejected because it is the ONLY
         suppression mechanism the ruleset configures. SuppressWarningsFilter,
         SuppressionCommentFilter and SuppressWithNearbyCommentFilter were all
         deliberately left out so that no exemption can be written into a Java
         source and hidden there; removing the file-based filter would leave a
         future exemption nowhere legitimate to live.

  Trade-offs: the empty form is chosen over pre-loading the exemptions the
    architecture can already be predicted to want — generated sources, test
    fixtures, Testcontainers boilerplate. Pre-loading would be shorter later and
    is rejected now because a suppression that exempts a path holding no files
    is unverifiable: nobody can tell whether it is still needed, whether its
    pattern is scoped as tightly as it should be, or whether it silently began
    matching hand-authored code. The accepted cost is that whoever first needs
    an exemption also adds the entry, which is the point at which its
    justification can actually be written truthfully.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
How to add an entry, and what it must carry.
  Each <suppress> element takes a "files" regular expression, optionally a
  "checks" or "id" regular expression, and optionally a line or column range.
  Omitting "checks" exempts the matched files from EVERY check in the ruleset,
  which is almost never what is wanted; name the narrowest check that is
  actually firing.

  Per the contract stated at the foot of checkstyle.xml, every entry added here
  carries three justifications of its own, in a comment immediately above it:
  why the exemption exists at all, how tightly its "files" pattern is scoped
  (and why it cannot match hand-authored source), and why each named check is
  the right one to relax rather than a wider family. That per-entry rationale is
  the whole reason exemptions are kept in one reviewable file instead of being
  scattered through the Java sources as annotations or magic comments.

  Two scopes are known NOT to need an entry, recorded so that a future reader
  does not add one defensively:
    - Non-Java files, including the fixed-width test fixtures under
      services/*/src/test/resources/fixtures/. checkstyle.xml sets
      fileExtensions="java", so nothing else is ever audited.
    - Maven build output under services/*/target/. The plugin audits the
      configured source roots only, so generated and compiled artifacts are
      already outside the audit set.
=============================================================================
  -->

<suppressions>

  <!--
    Deliberately empty. Add entries above this comment, newest last, each with
    the three justifications described in the header. Do not delete this element
    when the last entry is removed: the file must remain present and parseable,
    because the filter that reads it fails closed.
  -->

</suppressions>
